# TypeScript Type Safety

Zero tolerance for type safety escape hatches. Every violation found in this project eroded trust in the type system and hid real bugs.

---

## Zero `any`

### Rule
Never use `any` — not in variable types, function parameters, return types, generics, or type assertions.

### Forbidden Patterns

```ts
// BAD — file-wide eslint disable
/* eslint-disable @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any */

// BAD — any in function parameter
function handleEvent(data: any) { ... }

// BAD — any in type assertion
const result = value as any;

// BAD — any in generic
const items: Array<any> = [];
```

### What to Use Instead

| Instead of `any` | Use |
|---|---|
| Unknown data from external source | `unknown` + type guard |
| Flexible function parameter | Generic `<T>` with constraint |
| JSON parse result | `unknown` then validate |
| Event handler data | Typed event map (see `state-and-events.mdc`) |
| Third-party library without types | Write a `.d.ts` declaration |

### Type Guard Pattern

```ts
// GOOD — unknown + runtime validation
function handleSocketEvent(data: unknown): void {
  if (!isValidTurnInfo(data)) {
    Logger.error("Invalid turn info payload", data);
    return;
  }
  // data is now TurnInfo
  processTurnInfo(data);
}

function isValidTurnInfo(data: unknown): data is TurnInfo {
  return (
    typeof data === "object" &&
    data !== null &&
    "turnId" in data &&
    "playerId" in data
  );
}
```

